WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.360 --> 00:00:06.223
Welcome to Think Fast Talk Smart,
The Podcast.

00:00:06.223 --> 00:00:11.869
Think back to a day in your recent past,
did things go the way you expected?

00:00:11.869 --> 00:00:15.610
Did everything and
everyone follow the script you planned?

00:00:15.610 --> 00:00:19.948
Unfortunately, business and
life can be quite chaotic,

00:00:19.948 --> 00:00:22.522
unpredictable, and even messy.

00:00:22.522 --> 00:00:26.650
How can we better prepare ourselves
to manage all of this spontaneity

00:00:26.650 --> 00:00:28.583
in a creative, collegial way?

00:00:28.583 --> 00:00:30.275
Hi, I'm Matt Abrahams and

00:00:30.275 --> 00:00:34.993
I teach strategic communication at
Stanford Graduate School of Business.

00:00:34.993 --> 00:00:39.161
Today I am really looking forward
to speaking with Christian Wheeler,

00:00:39.161 --> 00:00:43.758
who is the GSB StrataCom professor of
management and professor of marketing.

00:00:43.758 --> 00:00:48.108
Christian teaches a variety of courses
including those on behavioral research,

00:00:48.108 --> 00:00:51.080
marketing management, and
spontaneous management.

00:00:51.080 --> 00:00:53.216
Welcome Christian.

00:00:53.216 --> 00:00:54.934
&gt;&gt; Hi Matt, great to be here.

00:00:54.934 --> 00:00:58.331
&gt;&gt; I've really enjoyed working with
you on a few projects at the GSB.

00:00:58.331 --> 00:01:03.064
You seem to have unending energy and
are amazing to watch teach.

00:01:03.064 --> 00:01:05.183
I'm so excited about our conversation.

00:01:05.183 --> 00:01:06.754
Should we get started?

00:01:06.754 --> 00:01:07.930
&gt;&gt; Yeah let's do it.

00:01:07.930 --> 00:01:10.352
&gt;&gt; All right,
as I mentioned in the beginning,

00:01:10.352 --> 00:01:12.345
things don't always go as planned.

00:01:12.345 --> 00:01:17.247
You teach a course in spontaneous
management, can you share with us two or

00:01:17.247 --> 00:01:20.977
three of the key learnings
you impart to your students.

00:01:20.977 --> 00:01:25.275
&gt;&gt; We all spend time making plans,
whether those are business plans such as

00:01:25.275 --> 00:01:29.927
a quarterly earnings, forecast or personal
plans such that we rehearse in our

00:01:29.927 --> 00:01:33.330
minds the way a conversation or
interaction is going to go.

00:01:33.330 --> 00:01:37.133
And things rarely turn out
exactly like we anticipated and

00:01:37.133 --> 00:01:41.488
that can be upsetting to people,
can throw them off of their game.

00:01:41.488 --> 00:01:46.105
And so, the class spontaneous
management is built around this idea of

00:01:46.105 --> 00:01:49.471
providing people a sense of calm and
flexibility so

00:01:49.471 --> 00:01:53.400
that they can adapt to whatever
circumstances come about.

00:01:53.400 --> 00:01:58.066
So, I know you talk a lot with your
students about managing anxiety.

00:01:58.066 --> 00:01:59.056
&gt;&gt; Yes we do.

00:01:59.056 --> 00:02:01.426
&gt;&gt; A lot of people feel [LAUGH] anxious,

00:02:01.426 --> 00:02:05.536
even giving a plan scripted
presentation in front of others.

00:02:05.536 --> 00:02:09.884
And how much worse is that when you're
dealing with circumstances that you

00:02:09.884 --> 00:02:12.714
can't completely control
such as a question and

00:02:12.714 --> 00:02:16.316
answer session that goes
differently than you anticipated.

00:02:16.316 --> 00:02:19.832
Or just being asked to make
spontaneous remarks about something.

00:02:19.832 --> 00:02:24.329
So one thing that we deal with right
at the beginning of the classes is just

00:02:24.329 --> 00:02:27.515
learning to be comfortable
being uncomfortable.

00:02:27.515 --> 00:02:32.659
All of us have some natural reaction
of arousal when we get in situations

00:02:32.659 --> 00:02:37.131
like that, and that's not
necessarily a good or a bad thing.

00:02:37.131 --> 00:02:40.558
What is good or bad is how we
respond to that sense of arousal.

00:02:40.558 --> 00:02:44.990
And so, sometimes when we get into
situations where we need to make unplanned

00:02:44.990 --> 00:02:47.322
comments, we can get a little anxious.

00:02:47.322 --> 00:02:49.616
And then we get anxious
about being anxious.

00:02:49.616 --> 00:02:50.179
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:02:50.179 --> 00:02:54.130
&gt;&gt; We feel bad about being anxious and
that's where the problem starts.

00:02:54.130 --> 00:02:57.032
There's nothing wrong
with being uncomfortable.

00:02:57.032 --> 00:03:01.490
The problem is with your
discomfort at being uncomfortable.

00:03:01.490 --> 00:03:05.376
And so we work on exercises teaching
people to be in that situation and

00:03:05.376 --> 00:03:08.193
to respond in that situation
with a sense of calm.

00:03:08.193 --> 00:03:11.336
They can acknowledge that they
feel a little bit anxious, but

00:03:11.336 --> 00:03:12.975
they're not going to dwell on it.

00:03:12.975 --> 00:03:15.580
And they're going to trust
their minds to bubble

00:03:15.580 --> 00:03:18.868
up the necessary responses
that they have in that moment.

00:03:18.868 --> 00:03:23.131
And it's when we start layering other
things on top of that feeling of arousal

00:03:23.131 --> 00:03:25.436
or anxiety that it becomes problematic.

00:03:25.436 --> 00:03:27.796
If you've ever watched American Football,

00:03:27.796 --> 00:03:31.715
they talk about how the best quarterbacks
have to have a short term memory.

00:03:31.715 --> 00:03:35.279
What that means is, if you go out and you
make a mistake, you throw an interception.

00:03:35.279 --> 00:03:36.831
The next time you go out there,

00:03:36.831 --> 00:03:40.078
you can't be thinking about that
interception you just threw.

00:03:40.078 --> 00:03:42.225
You need to be thinking
about in the moment,

00:03:42.225 --> 00:03:45.893
what you need to be doing to adapt to
that next series of plays that you have.

00:03:45.893 --> 00:03:49.605
And so, the less time you can
spend dwelling on your mistakes,

00:03:49.605 --> 00:03:54.312
the more mental energy you can devote to
doing what you need to do in that moment.

00:03:54.312 --> 00:03:56.088
&gt;&gt; So I really liked that advice.

00:03:56.088 --> 00:04:00.319
And a lot of this is just acknowledging
that getting nervous in those types

00:04:00.319 --> 00:04:04.893
of situations is normal and natural,
rather than something that is bizarre and

00:04:04.893 --> 00:04:05.519
strange.

00:04:05.519 --> 00:04:07.481
Most people get that way and

00:04:07.481 --> 00:04:13.031
then that can help short circuit some
of that anxiety you're talking about.

00:04:13.031 --> 00:04:18.102
Do you work with your students on specific
things they can do to develop that short

00:04:18.102 --> 00:04:23.042
term memory and to help reduce some of
that feeling bad about feeling nervous?

00:04:23.042 --> 00:04:24.169
&gt;&gt; Yeah, absolutely.

00:04:24.169 --> 00:04:26.181
So I guess that was going to
bring me to the second thing.

00:04:26.181 --> 00:04:29.461
We work on having a sense
of not fearing failure.

00:04:29.461 --> 00:04:34.470
So failure is another thing,
you need a similar set of responses.

00:04:34.470 --> 00:04:38.462
Failure is not something that we seek and
no one wants to fail, but

00:04:38.462 --> 00:04:40.785
the fear of failure can create these,

00:04:40.785 --> 00:04:44.646
layer all of these other maladaptive
responses on top of that.

00:04:44.646 --> 00:04:47.741
And so what we do is we put
people into situations,

00:04:47.741 --> 00:04:52.104
some exercises we do early on or
what improvisers called brain fries.

00:04:52.104 --> 00:04:56.964
They're situations where your brain
simply can't compute all of the necessary

00:04:56.964 --> 00:04:57.900
information.

00:04:57.900 --> 00:05:01.299
And you learn to be in those
situations with a sense of comfort,

00:05:01.299 --> 00:05:02.849
at least relative comfort.

00:05:02.849 --> 00:05:06.492
And you learn to treat failure
as something that's okay.

00:05:06.492 --> 00:05:10.815
Here in Silicon Valley we have this
mantra, many companies do of failing fast,

00:05:10.815 --> 00:05:12.630
failing quickly and all of that.

00:05:12.630 --> 00:05:15.863
And that's fine as
an intellectual orientation, but

00:05:15.863 --> 00:05:19.307
many of us when we're confronted
with personal failure,

00:05:19.307 --> 00:05:22.979
even with things that are not high stakes,
it feels bad to us.

00:05:22.979 --> 00:05:25.581
And we work hard to try
to avoid that failure and

00:05:25.581 --> 00:05:27.796
that can often be counterproductive.

00:05:27.796 --> 00:05:32.507
So we try to encourage people to accept
failure as being something that's okay.

00:05:32.507 --> 00:05:38.304
And to enter situations like that with
a sense of play and a sense of joy.

00:05:38.304 --> 00:05:41.150
&gt;&gt; I once heard a wonderful get of advice.

00:05:41.150 --> 00:05:45.188
That, just because you fail,
doesn't mean you're a failure.

00:05:45.188 --> 00:05:47.856
Failing can actually be
a wonderful learning opportunity.

00:05:47.856 --> 00:05:52.723
But it sound like a what you are trying to
inculcate in your students is this idea

00:05:52.723 --> 00:05:54.365
that failing isn't bad.

00:05:54.365 --> 00:05:58.535
And it's something you can
actually desensitize yourself to.

00:05:58.535 --> 00:06:01.450
&gt;&gt; Failing is something that
is on the pathway to success.

00:06:01.450 --> 00:06:04.367
People forget what it was
like to be a young child,

00:06:04.367 --> 00:06:06.318
children are bad at everything.

00:06:06.318 --> 00:06:08.216
[LAUGH] They don't know how to walk.

00:06:08.216 --> 00:06:10.247
They don't know how to use a spoon.

00:06:10.247 --> 00:06:12.769
They don't know how to tie their shoes,
they can't do anything.

00:06:12.769 --> 00:06:16.496
And children fail and fail and
fail and fail and fail and

00:06:16.496 --> 00:06:18.616
fail until they stop failing.

00:06:18.616 --> 00:06:22.768
And so a young person is used to
failing all the time, it's no big deal.

00:06:22.768 --> 00:06:23.864
And through failure,

00:06:23.864 --> 00:06:27.338
they learn how to gain the skills that
they need to participate in life.

00:06:27.338 --> 00:06:32.222
And as adults we get to some point where
we can structure our own environments in

00:06:32.222 --> 00:06:36.218
such a way that we don't put
ourselves in failure situations and

00:06:36.218 --> 00:06:38.441
that's where we stopped growing.

00:06:38.441 --> 00:06:42.424
We need to recognize that failure is a
great thing because failure suggests that

00:06:42.424 --> 00:06:44.930
we are operating at
the outside of our abilities.

00:06:44.930 --> 00:06:49.382
And that we have some skills that we
can acquire it to better adapt to our

00:06:49.382 --> 00:06:50.517
circumstances.

00:06:50.517 --> 00:06:55.735
&gt;&gt; You remind me of a company I worked for
many years ago, we had failure Fridays.

00:06:55.735 --> 00:06:59.368
And the CEO essentially rewarded
people for stepping forward and

00:06:59.368 --> 00:07:01.819
acknowledging some work failure they had.

00:07:01.819 --> 00:07:04.729
With the idea of being one,
what you're saying,

00:07:04.729 --> 00:07:07.375
there is to push ourselves
to try new things.

00:07:07.375 --> 00:07:09.992
And of course, when you try new things,
failure happens.

00:07:09.992 --> 00:07:12.091
But also to be an educational experience.

00:07:12.091 --> 00:07:15.420
So it was okay to fail, but
let's not make the same mistake again.

00:07:15.420 --> 00:07:19.877
So those failure Fridays were actually
quite fun and people would compete to have

00:07:19.877 --> 00:07:23.034
the biggest failure, and
it turned into quite an event.

00:07:23.034 --> 00:07:27.321
But it was really something that helped
desensitize people to failure and

00:07:27.321 --> 00:07:28.645
encourage learning.

00:07:28.645 --> 00:07:29.837
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I love that idea.

00:07:29.837 --> 00:07:30.740
I love that idea and

00:07:30.740 --> 00:07:33.860
then everyone else can learn from
your failures as well, right?

00:07:33.860 --> 00:07:37.658
They learn what didn't work and and
it destigmatizes your own failure,

00:07:37.658 --> 00:07:38.536
that's great.

00:07:38.536 --> 00:07:39.672
&gt;&gt; Absolutely.

00:07:39.672 --> 00:07:44.223
So in your course you talk
about creative collaboration.

00:07:44.223 --> 00:07:48.276
Can you provide us with some
practical tips on how we can be

00:07:48.276 --> 00:07:50.985
more creative in our collaboration?

00:07:50.985 --> 00:07:51.696
&gt;&gt; Sure, so,

00:07:51.696 --> 00:07:56.269
one of the things we work on in my
courses is understanding your tendencies.

00:07:56.269 --> 00:08:00.910
So all of us have We exist
along some continuum

00:08:00.910 --> 00:08:04.784
of either wanting to seize control or
wanting to avoid control.

00:08:04.784 --> 00:08:08.237
And so we do some exercises aimed at
helping you understand your basic

00:08:08.237 --> 00:08:08.958
tendencies.

00:08:08.958 --> 00:08:13.390
And then within that to recognize that
the things that your collaboration

00:08:13.390 --> 00:08:15.540
partners are giving you are gifts.

00:08:15.540 --> 00:08:20.893
So what a lot of people don't fully
appreciate about the creative

00:08:20.893 --> 00:08:26.360
process is the value of inputs that
come outside of your own brain.

00:08:26.360 --> 00:08:27.927
Whether they're from your environment,

00:08:27.927 --> 00:08:30.631
or whether they're from someone else
that you're collaborating with.

00:08:30.631 --> 00:08:34.887
But are there Kessler talks about this as
having intersecting reality planes, right?

00:08:34.887 --> 00:08:39.484
We have two different ways of viewing the
world, each of us has our own perspective,

00:08:39.484 --> 00:08:42.677
our own set of experiences,
our own set of associations.

00:08:42.677 --> 00:08:46.417
And that when those things intersect
that's when you can have interesting

00:08:46.417 --> 00:08:47.555
creative solutions.

00:08:47.555 --> 00:08:51.107
And so when we're working alone we can
do things to foster that process, but

00:08:51.107 --> 00:08:54.234
the collaborative process naturally
creates that type of thing.

00:08:54.234 --> 00:08:57.722
And so one thing we work on
is viewing inputs as gifts or

00:08:57.722 --> 00:09:02.950
as an improviser would say as an offer
as something to be built upon.

00:09:02.950 --> 00:09:07.878
And so if you've read anything or heard
anything about him from improvisational

00:09:07.878 --> 00:09:10.278
theatre that the first rule is yes and.

00:09:10.278 --> 00:09:13.817
And so what we do is we affirm
what the person gave us and

00:09:13.817 --> 00:09:16.267
then we build directly on that idea.

00:09:16.267 --> 00:09:20.576
And what people who do these exercises
with me find is that they come up with

00:09:20.576 --> 00:09:22.393
crazy innovative solutions.

00:09:22.393 --> 00:09:25.821
That they never would have been able to
dream up on their own just by having that

00:09:25.821 --> 00:09:30.951
additional person provided input into them
&gt;&gt; So do you teach your students or

00:09:30.951 --> 00:09:34.960
do you have some recommendations for
some ground rules or

00:09:34.960 --> 00:09:39.147
some direct that can help people
feel okay being creative?

00:09:39.147 --> 00:09:43.645
And saying yes and there's a lot of
pressure to get things right and

00:09:43.645 --> 00:09:46.874
I feel like that stifles this yes and
approach.

00:09:46.874 --> 00:09:49.692
&gt;&gt; Yeah, absolutely, so
I think what's important and

00:09:49.692 --> 00:09:51.633
the sessions I teach on creativity,

00:09:51.633 --> 00:09:56.160
it's important to know which stage of the
creative solving process that you're in.

00:09:57.210 --> 00:10:01.124
We often have a tendency to judge our
ideas at the same time we're creating them

00:10:01.124 --> 00:10:03.984
and that's something that
humans are not good at doing.

00:10:03.984 --> 00:10:07.636
There's a time to judge but that's not
the time when you're creating ideas,

00:10:07.636 --> 00:10:09.790
you can judge ideas later.

00:10:09.790 --> 00:10:12.195
&gt;&gt; So
you essentially encourage people to one,

00:10:12.195 --> 00:10:15.690
recognize that we're in
the brainstorming and ideation phase.

00:10:15.690 --> 00:10:19.810
Or we're in a different phase, and
in that ideation phase, anything goes.

00:10:19.810 --> 00:10:24.664
Let's not judge, let's really focus on
how we can see what others are saying or

00:10:24.664 --> 00:10:25.250
offers.

00:10:25.250 --> 00:10:28.555
&gt;&gt; Yes and to build upon those things,
because we don't know the value of

00:10:28.555 --> 00:10:31.630
an idea until we follow it
to its logical conclusion.

00:10:31.630 --> 00:10:34.877
And even if we end up someplace
that isn't super valuable for us,

00:10:34.877 --> 00:10:38.950
we may find that there are aspects of that
idea that are still valuable that we could

00:10:38.950 --> 00:10:40.621
incorporate in a different way.

00:10:40.621 --> 00:10:44.471
&gt;&gt; Certainly and I can only imagine
that in interacting with others in

00:10:44.471 --> 00:10:48.471
that way collaborating in that way
it builds trust and camaraderie.

00:10:48.471 --> 00:10:50.423
So that perhaps that idea wasn't good but

00:10:50.423 --> 00:10:52.774
it might lead to the creation
of other good ideas.

00:10:52.774 --> 00:10:54.754
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I think that's exactly right,

00:10:54.754 --> 00:10:58.896
people are thinking a lot more about
issues of diversity, equity and Inclusion.

00:10:58.896 --> 00:11:03.609
And I think that's really important to
think about in any given context you may

00:11:03.609 --> 00:11:06.253
be an individual who
feels that you belong or

00:11:06.253 --> 00:11:08.897
feels that your
contributions are valued or

00:11:08.897 --> 00:11:13.898
feel like you have something useful to say
And the way people respond to your initial

00:11:13.898 --> 00:11:18.848
ideas are probably going to affect the way
that you interact in that environment.

00:11:18.848 --> 00:11:23.780
And what I mean by that is you mentioned
the valuation apprehension associated with

00:11:23.780 --> 00:11:25.901
a group decision context, right?

00:11:25.901 --> 00:11:30.099
I don't want to say something stupid or be
judged for that and I have some threshold

00:11:30.099 --> 00:11:33.944
for how good I think an idea is before
I'm going to let it come out of my mouth.

00:11:33.944 --> 00:11:37.482
And then I'm going to look to people's
responses to that and if that initial

00:11:37.482 --> 00:11:41.428
response is negative the next time around
that threshold may be a little bit higher.

00:11:41.428 --> 00:11:45.344
And so it's very easy to unintentionally
stifle the voices of people

00:11:45.344 --> 00:11:48.720
who may have varying levels
of comfort with contributing,

00:11:48.720 --> 00:11:52.390
because they feel that they
belong in that group or don't.

00:11:52.390 --> 00:11:56.864
One thing that this orientation does is it
makes sure that you're hearing all voices

00:11:56.864 --> 00:12:00.471
even those who may be a little more
reluctant to speak up initially.

00:12:00.471 --> 00:12:04.269
&gt;&gt; And that is so critical for not only
good decision making and feeling part

00:12:04.269 --> 00:12:08.139
of a group but just for furthering
this cause of diversity and inclusion.

00:12:08.139 --> 00:12:12.886
Beyond focusing on spontaneity and
management you did some research on

00:12:12.886 --> 00:12:16.456
what you referred to as
positive implying pronouns.

00:12:16.456 --> 00:12:21.339
Can you give us the scoop on how our
language use specifically pronouns

00:12:21.339 --> 00:12:25.823
might help us in our communication
&gt;&gt; Sure so the pronouns that we use

00:12:25.823 --> 00:12:30.609
indicate something about the relationships
that we have with others or

00:12:30.609 --> 00:12:32.614
the norms of this situation.

00:12:32.614 --> 00:12:36.525
So that research was about the language
that businesses use to communicate

00:12:36.525 --> 00:12:37.710
to customers.

00:12:37.710 --> 00:12:41.701
So if I'm in a joint business venture
with somebody loosely speaking,

00:12:41.701 --> 00:12:45.771
meaning that I'm a banker trying to
communicate to a potential client.

00:12:45.771 --> 00:12:49.820
Or I'm an insurance company trying to
communicate to a potential client,

00:12:49.820 --> 00:12:52.890
how do I talk about that relationship?

00:12:52.890 --> 00:12:55.471
One way we could phrase that
as I could say, you and

00:12:55.471 --> 00:12:58.128
I will work together to
meet your financial needs.

00:12:58.128 --> 00:13:02.920
Or I could say we will work together
to meet those financial needs.

00:13:02.920 --> 00:13:06.841
And what research shows is that
when people use pronouns like we,

00:13:06.841 --> 00:13:08.853
that is indicating implicitly.

00:13:08.853 --> 00:13:12.047
But in a way that people pick up
on it's indicating closeness and

00:13:12.047 --> 00:13:13.550
a shared identity.

00:13:13.550 --> 00:13:17.408
So if you look at committed
relationship partners they use

00:13:17.408 --> 00:13:19.543
the word we more often than not.

00:13:19.543 --> 00:13:24.366
We, our and us, this pronoun
that implies this togetherness

00:13:24.366 --> 00:13:28.950
versus separateness pronouns such as I,
me or you.

00:13:28.950 --> 00:13:32.381
And if you look at the spontaneous
language of couples,

00:13:32.381 --> 00:13:35.520
couples who use these
inclusive pronouns like we,

00:13:35.520 --> 00:13:39.178
have higher relationship
satisfaction than once we use I.

00:13:39.178 --> 00:13:44.200
And so it can be an indicator of
the type of relationship that you have.

00:13:44.200 --> 00:13:48.689
I mean it can also increase relationship
satisfaction when you tend to use those

00:13:48.689 --> 00:13:49.364
pronouns.

00:13:49.364 --> 00:13:52.862
But how successful that will be will
depend on the existing norms of

00:13:52.862 --> 00:13:53.741
the situation.

00:13:53.741 --> 00:13:57.999
So in a business context, what our
research showed is that there are certain

00:13:57.999 --> 00:14:02.205
industries for which people have
expectations of a closer relationship.

00:14:02.205 --> 00:14:07.009
And other industries where they feel they
have an expectation of a more distant

00:14:07.009 --> 00:14:08.040
relationship.

00:14:08.040 --> 00:14:12.716
And that people responded more positively
when the pronouns used reflected those

00:14:12.716 --> 00:14:15.435
existing norms that people
had in their minds.

00:14:15.435 --> 00:14:19.896
We also know that the use of the pronoun
you just referring to you as you can

00:14:19.896 --> 00:14:23.350
increase what psychologists
calls self referencing.

00:14:23.350 --> 00:14:27.214
Which means that you are relating
the information to yourself, so

00:14:27.214 --> 00:14:31.157
if I say something like computer
technology is changing every day.

00:14:31.157 --> 00:14:35.106
Or I could say Matt you know the computer
technology is changing every day.

00:14:35.106 --> 00:14:40.081
Just adding that you there makes you more
likely to think about that information

00:14:40.081 --> 00:14:41.575
in a self relevant way.

00:14:41.575 --> 00:14:44.326
Just along the lines of
using someone's name,

00:14:44.326 --> 00:14:47.506
which relates to some other
research that I've done.

00:14:47.506 --> 00:14:48.699
&gt;&gt; Interesting, so

00:14:48.699 --> 00:14:53.159
using you using someone's name
invites people to pay attention.

00:14:53.159 --> 00:14:57.600
And then do some self reflection in a way
that they might not if you don't use that

00:14:57.600 --> 00:14:58.277
language.

00:14:58.277 --> 00:15:02.216
&gt;&gt; That's right, yes we recently
published a paper showing that just

00:15:02.216 --> 00:15:04.773
putting someone's name in the subject line

00:15:04.773 --> 00:15:08.600
of an email even when they know
that you already knew their name.

00:15:08.600 --> 00:15:12.464
Increases their likelihood of opening
the email and increases the likelihood of

00:15:12.464 --> 00:15:16.003
generating sales lead or having them
click through to it to another page.

00:15:16.003 --> 00:15:19.886
And interestingly it decreases
unsubscribes and the way it has this

00:15:19.886 --> 00:15:24.397
effect is through increasing elaboration
of the information in the message.

00:15:24.397 --> 00:15:27.696
So if you provide compelling
reason to adopt the product or

00:15:27.696 --> 00:15:32.339
to browse on the webpage then they're more
likely to do that when you use their name

00:15:32.339 --> 00:15:33.572
in the subject line.

00:15:33.572 --> 00:15:37.367
Even again, when they know that you know
their names such as with Stanford alums,

00:15:37.367 --> 00:15:39.820
or an existing customer of a company.

00:15:39.820 --> 00:15:44.206
&gt;&gt; Fascinating, you talked about and
we talked about this notion of rapport and

00:15:44.206 --> 00:15:46.511
bonding and feeling part of something.

00:15:46.511 --> 00:15:49.785
It goes without saying that teams
that know each other well tend

00:15:49.785 --> 00:15:51.590
to work better together.

00:15:51.590 --> 00:15:54.846
Unfortunately, we don't always have
time to bond with our colleagues.

00:15:54.846 --> 00:15:57.532
Or perhaps with our new
ubiquitous remote work,

00:15:57.532 --> 00:16:02.350
we don't have the benefit of in-person for
lack of a better term schmoozing.

00:16:02.350 --> 00:16:06.571
I've seen you help teams bond rapidly,
what advice and guidance can

00:16:06.571 --> 00:16:11.790
you provide to help us get to know our
coworkers better and more quickly?

00:16:11.790 --> 00:16:14.260
&gt;&gt; You raised a really good issue there.

00:16:14.260 --> 00:16:18.615
This remote working thing is dramatically
changing the way that organizations

00:16:18.615 --> 00:16:19.787
function, I think.

00:16:19.787 --> 00:16:21.299
So one fear that people had,

00:16:21.299 --> 00:16:25.709
if people are coming into the office and
I'm not there to look over their shoulder,

00:16:25.709 --> 00:16:28.750
maybe I'm worried they're not
going to get any work done.

00:16:28.750 --> 00:16:30.830
That turns out, from what I've seen,
not to be true.

00:16:30.830 --> 00:16:33.321
People are certainly capable
of supervising themselves and

00:16:33.321 --> 00:16:34.160
getting work done.

00:16:34.160 --> 00:16:37.858
But what we do miss is this
contact that we have with people

00:16:37.858 --> 00:16:42.186
stopping by their office or
running into them at the water cooler.

00:16:42.186 --> 00:16:46.761
These little interactions with one another
have a huge effect on the level of

00:16:46.761 --> 00:16:49.130
bonding that we feel with one another.

00:16:49.130 --> 00:16:52.397
So we know that proximity for example
is one of the biggest predictors of

00:16:52.397 --> 00:16:55.294
attraction, that just means how
close we are to individuals.

00:16:55.294 --> 00:16:59.684
You're more likely to be friends with
someone who has an office or cubicle next

00:16:59.684 --> 00:17:03.750
door to you than someone who has
an office or cubicle on the second floor.

00:17:03.750 --> 00:17:07.114
And also, there's this principle
developed by Bob Zajonc, who was

00:17:07.114 --> 00:17:11.230
a Stanford psychology professor before
he passed away called mere-exposure.

00:17:11.230 --> 00:17:15.350
Simply coming into contact with something
repeatedly makes us like it more.

00:17:15.350 --> 00:17:18.933
And so these little interactions,
that we may not feel that they're

00:17:18.933 --> 00:17:22.024
a critical component of our
organizational functioning,

00:17:22.024 --> 00:17:24.824
have a huge effect on how
close we feel to one another.

00:17:24.824 --> 00:17:28.486
And when we have those frequent and
positive interactions,

00:17:28.486 --> 00:17:30.725
they form the foundation for trust.

00:17:30.725 --> 00:17:34.967
And the foundation of trust helps us
identify more with our organization and

00:17:34.967 --> 00:17:37.360
the organizational mission.

00:17:37.360 --> 00:17:40.980
And what we've seen here with meetings,
for example, is that we often think of

00:17:40.980 --> 00:17:44.555
the purpose of meetings is just to be
the things that are on the agenda sheet.

00:17:44.555 --> 00:17:47.823
But what we see is that meetings have
a lot of other functions, they serve

00:17:47.823 --> 00:17:51.534
the socialization functions, even if it's
not an explicit part of the meeting.

00:17:51.534 --> 00:17:56.165
We show up to the table a few minutes
early or linger a few minutes afterwards.

00:17:56.165 --> 00:18:00.390
And we have this incidental contact with
others that is taken away when we're

00:18:00.390 --> 00:18:02.890
working in this remote environment.

00:18:02.890 --> 00:18:07.299
One thing that organizations need to think
about is ways to artificially induce

00:18:07.299 --> 00:18:11.950
that into this more formal Zoom style
interaction that we've adopted.

00:18:11.950 --> 00:18:15.973
So in my exercises what we do is we help
people engage in exercises where they're

00:18:15.973 --> 00:18:20.430
interacting with one another,
they're experiencing this positive affect.

00:18:20.430 --> 00:18:23.290
And they're engaging in some
degree of self-disclosure,

00:18:23.290 --> 00:18:26.520
which is something that creates
the foundations of trust.

00:18:26.520 --> 00:18:30.700
&gt;&gt; Can you give an example, one or
two examples of an activity that you do?

00:18:30.700 --> 00:18:33.460
I've seen you do multiple ones,
I'm curious if one or

00:18:33.460 --> 00:18:35.920
two bubbled to the top
that you'd like to share.

00:18:37.710 --> 00:18:39.180
&gt;&gt; Yeah, they can be very,

00:18:39.180 --> 00:18:42.760
very simple things such as
minor acts of self-disclosure.

00:18:42.760 --> 00:18:46.937
I have people, for example, psychologists
have developed these series of

00:18:46.937 --> 00:18:50.020
questions to create rapport
in the lab artificially.

00:18:50.020 --> 00:18:54.429
So oftentimes, people may want to study
closer versus more distant relationships.

00:18:54.429 --> 00:18:57.809
But when you just bring friends
versus strangers into the lab,

00:18:57.809 --> 00:18:59.670
there are all kinds of confounds.

00:18:59.670 --> 00:19:03.938
But what they've developed is a series
of questions with escalating levels of

00:19:03.938 --> 00:19:07.949
self-disclosure that can create these
feelings of a close relationship

00:19:07.949 --> 00:19:11.132
even with someone that you've
met just mere minutes ago.

00:19:11.132 --> 00:19:14.175
And so one thing we do is we have bring
people through those questions and

00:19:14.175 --> 00:19:17.474
they escalate in a way such that people
generally feel comfortable revealing

00:19:17.474 --> 00:19:19.300
something about themselves.

00:19:19.300 --> 00:19:23.421
So it's not too fast, but it's faster than
you probably would normally in an ordinary

00:19:23.421 --> 00:19:24.837
conversation with someone.

00:19:24.837 --> 00:19:27.210
&gt;&gt; So give me an example
of one of those questions.

00:19:28.260 --> 00:19:30.503
&gt;&gt; Well,
you start out with things such as, Matt,

00:19:30.503 --> 00:19:32.250
how long have you been at Stanford?

00:19:32.250 --> 00:19:34.418
Why did you come here, this type of thing,

00:19:34.418 --> 00:19:38.105
moving through a series of questions
in a series of stages to get to more.

00:19:38.105 --> 00:19:42.291
The last level, we're talking about things
like an embarrassing experience that you

00:19:42.291 --> 00:19:46.533
learned from, or something you really want
to accomplish in the next 20 years that's

00:19:46.533 --> 00:19:47.820
close to your heart.

00:19:47.820 --> 00:19:51.840
&gt;&gt; So it's a gradual escalation where
each person reveals a little bit so

00:19:51.840 --> 00:19:54.750
they feel more connected as they go.

00:19:54.750 --> 00:19:58.831
&gt;&gt; That's right and what we've seen
through research is that, we like people

00:19:58.831 --> 00:20:03.700
who self-disclose to us and we like people
more when we self-disclose to them.

00:20:03.700 --> 00:20:08.393
&gt;&gt; This notion of self-disclosure in a
virtual world, I've done this with some of

00:20:08.393 --> 00:20:12.809
the students I teach and some of the folks
I coach, where we'll just say take

00:20:12.809 --> 00:20:17.430
something in the room you're sitting
in and just show it to us virtually.

00:20:17.430 --> 00:20:19.507
It's like show and
tell when we were kids, but

00:20:19.507 --> 00:20:22.720
it's a way of disclosure because people
will explain why it's important.

00:20:22.720 --> 00:20:26.977
It also has this wonderful side effect of
making people turn on their videos and

00:20:26.977 --> 00:20:29.250
people are loathed to do that sometimes.

00:20:29.250 --> 00:20:31.690
So it feeds into what you're discussing.

00:20:31.690 --> 00:20:35.524
&gt;&gt; Yeah, that's an interesting thing that
we see with Zoom meetings now is that we

00:20:35.524 --> 00:20:39.374
get glimpses into people's lives that
we wouldn't ordinarily have, right?

00:20:39.374 --> 00:20:43.046
We see their living rooms and
their children and their pets and so

00:20:43.046 --> 00:20:48.012
people are often engaging in unintentional
forms of self-disclosure that still give

00:20:48.012 --> 00:20:51.130
us the feeling of knowing
that person better.

00:20:51.130 --> 00:20:51.830
&gt;&gt; Absolutely,

00:20:51.830 --> 00:20:56.300
I think you can connect to people much
more when you see more of them, for sure.

00:20:56.300 --> 00:20:59.957
I'd like to move on and I know that you
use improv games to help your students

00:20:59.957 --> 00:21:02.120
better understand the concepts you teach.

00:21:02.120 --> 00:21:05.570
And I'm wondering if you'd be willing
to play a quick improv game that I

00:21:05.570 --> 00:21:06.500
just made up.

00:21:06.500 --> 00:21:07.276
&gt;&gt; All right, let's do it.
&gt;&gt; Okay,

00:21:07.276 --> 00:21:10.211
I'm going to name a business activity and
I'd like for

00:21:10.211 --> 00:21:13.555
you to respond with the first
thing that comes to your mind.

00:21:13.555 --> 00:21:14.080
&gt;&gt; Sure.

00:21:14.080 --> 00:21:15.902
&gt;&gt; All right, here we go, email.

00:21:15.902 --> 00:21:20.300
&gt;&gt; Hell [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] Tell me why,

00:21:20.300 --> 00:21:25.270
what are your thoughts on email and
any advice on how to make it better?

00:21:25.270 --> 00:21:27.900
&gt;&gt; Email is a bad form of communication.

00:21:27.900 --> 00:21:32.069
I mean, some of it is our own fault, for
example, I'm not as good as I could be at

00:21:32.069 --> 00:21:36.148
managing my inboxes and keeping personal
things separate from work things.

00:21:36.148 --> 00:21:41.151
But even within a work inbox, there is
some things that are very important,

00:21:41.151 --> 00:21:43.191
things that are unimportant.

00:21:43.191 --> 00:21:46.860
And so one thing we can do as senders for
example is to clearly indicate in

00:21:46.860 --> 00:21:50.777
the subject line whether this is something
that requires action on my part,

00:21:50.777 --> 00:21:53.899
whether this is something that's just for
my information,

00:21:53.899 --> 00:21:56.560
whether this is a newsletter or
that type of thing.

00:21:56.560 --> 00:22:00.899
Because we end up in this endless task
without clear prioritization, there

00:22:00.899 --> 00:22:05.867
are various technological solutions that
can help with that but nothing's perfect.

00:22:05.867 --> 00:22:11.394
And then also, emails tend to be too long,
they're not prioritized efficiently and so

00:22:11.394 --> 00:22:15.035
it's easy to start drowning in this swamp-
&gt;&gt; Absolutely.

00:22:15.035 --> 00:22:16.437
&gt;&gt; Of email.

00:22:16.437 --> 00:22:20.468
&gt;&gt; We, on this podcast, we've talked in
the past about how structure can really

00:22:20.468 --> 00:22:23.474
help you when you're trying
to be efficient and concise.

00:22:23.474 --> 00:22:27.566
And just thinking about how to structure
an email rather than just listing

00:22:27.566 --> 00:22:29.353
bullet points can really help.

00:22:29.353 --> 00:22:33.645
So I agree, being more concise and
direct is helpful.

00:22:33.645 --> 00:22:38.406
Let me give you the last activity I'd like
your thoughts on, performance reviews

00:22:38.406 --> 00:22:41.486
&gt;&gt; Approach.

00:22:41.486 --> 00:22:45.320
&gt;&gt; Tell me more what you mean about that.

00:22:45.320 --> 00:22:48.015
&gt;&gt; I think it's incredibly
useful to seek feedback and

00:22:48.015 --> 00:22:50.904
I think most of us tend not to
do that as much as we should.

00:22:50.904 --> 00:22:55.630
When we think about performance review,
when we're on the receiving end of that,

00:22:55.630 --> 00:23:00.080
that's often a source of evaluation
apprehension or anxiety.

00:23:00.080 --> 00:23:01.791
When we're on the giving end of that,

00:23:01.791 --> 00:23:04.990
that can often be the source of
uncomfortable conversations.

00:23:04.990 --> 00:23:09.198
But the only way you learn how you're
doing is through seeking performance

00:23:09.198 --> 00:23:09.880
feedback.

00:23:09.880 --> 00:23:13.810
And I think people should do it
more frequently than they do.

00:23:13.810 --> 00:23:17.295
And again, if they have this orientation
that receiving negative feedback is

00:23:17.295 --> 00:23:20.936
something on the pathway to growth, then
that can take some of the potential sting

00:23:20.936 --> 00:23:24.420
out of hearing news that you
don't necessarily want to hear.

00:23:24.420 --> 00:23:26.887
&gt;&gt; I like that,
that approach really does change.

00:23:26.887 --> 00:23:29.198
And it feeds right back
into what you talked about,

00:23:29.198 --> 00:23:30.820
seeing things as offers and gifts.

00:23:30.820 --> 00:23:33.569
I mean, in some ways,
performance reviews are gifts,

00:23:33.569 --> 00:23:36.320
they're tools to help you better yourself.

00:23:36.320 --> 00:23:41.100
Just taking that mindset versus, I'm being
judged evaluated, I have to defend myself.

00:23:41.100 --> 00:23:43.980
That could really change
how these things go.

00:23:43.980 --> 00:23:49.530
&gt;&gt; Yeah, absolutely, and talking to all of
the communications lectures at Stanford,

00:23:49.530 --> 00:23:51.620
that set is fantastic at doing.

00:23:51.620 --> 00:23:55.263
They continually seek student feedback in
an open-ended way and they continually

00:23:55.263 --> 00:23:58.460
revise their class so that they can
better meet the needs of the students.

00:23:58.460 --> 00:23:59.787
I'm blushing Christian.

00:23:59.787 --> 00:24:00.328
Thank you.

00:24:00.328 --> 00:24:02.772
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Before we end,

00:24:02.772 --> 00:24:06.707
I like to ask the same three questions
of everybody that I speak with and

00:24:06.707 --> 00:24:08.789
I'm hoping you'll join me in this.

00:24:08.789 --> 00:24:09.823
&gt;&gt; Sure.

00:24:09.823 --> 00:24:15.575
&gt;&gt; If you would capture the best
communication advice, you ever received,

00:24:15.575 --> 00:24:20.879
as a five to seven word presentation
slide title, what would it be?

00:24:20.879 --> 00:24:24.173
&gt;&gt; Five words,
I would say make a connection with others.

00:24:24.173 --> 00:24:25.515
&gt;&gt; Okay, tell me more.

00:24:25.515 --> 00:24:29.510
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] Well, that sounds a little
trade on the surface of it, but

00:24:29.510 --> 00:24:32.841
there are lots of things that
we do that can increase or

00:24:32.841 --> 00:24:35.374
decrease our likelihood of doing that.

00:24:35.374 --> 00:24:39.813
When I first came to Stanford we had
communication coaches that that help us

00:24:39.813 --> 00:24:44.124
improve our teaching and I got some
fabulous advice that I never forgot.

00:24:44.124 --> 00:24:47.974
One is I used to wander around
a lot when I was speaking.

00:24:47.974 --> 00:24:51.969
I don't know if it was nervous energy or
I thought it was kicky.

00:24:51.969 --> 00:24:54.030
It tends to be very distracting for
people.

00:24:54.030 --> 00:24:56.546
They're moving their heads around
like they're watching a tennis match.

00:24:56.546 --> 00:24:58.680
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; The communications instructor said to

00:24:58.680 --> 00:25:00.766
me I want you to hold on to
this podium and don't let go.

00:25:00.766 --> 00:25:01.770
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:25:01.770 --> 00:25:03.926
&gt;&gt; I want you to just stand right here and

00:25:03.926 --> 00:25:04.761
do not move and

00:25:04.761 --> 00:25:08.884
when you do that when you can
deliberately use Stillness in your body.

00:25:08.884 --> 00:25:11.281
&gt;&gt; Uh-huh.
&gt;&gt; It gives you incredible power as

00:25:11.281 --> 00:25:14.632
a communicator,
when you can move with intention and

00:25:14.632 --> 00:25:16.579
also not move with intention.

00:25:16.579 --> 00:25:20.009
So you can create this gravity and
stillness when you want to and

00:25:20.009 --> 00:25:21.575
then you can create a break.

00:25:21.575 --> 00:25:25.449
Another thing that person said is to make
extended eye contact with people, that's

00:25:25.449 --> 00:25:29.399
something I didn't bring up in the bonding
thing that we were talking about earlier.

00:25:29.399 --> 00:25:31.297
&gt;&gt; Right.
&gt;&gt; But what this coach said, she says,

00:25:31.297 --> 00:25:34.307
look, I want you to just pick one person
in the audience and look at them for

00:25:34.307 --> 00:25:34.957
a few seconds.

00:25:34.957 --> 00:25:37.722
And then I want you to look at
another person, pick them and

00:25:37.722 --> 00:25:39.873
make eye contact with them for
a few seconds.

00:25:39.873 --> 00:25:42.071
So she said, don't do the general sweep or

00:25:42.071 --> 00:25:46.058
look into the far distance as though
you're watching a sunset in their eyes.

00:25:46.058 --> 00:25:49.453
And then she said, I want you
to pick specific individuals and

00:25:49.453 --> 00:25:51.557
make extended eye contact with them.

00:25:51.557 --> 00:25:55.216
And what you find is that person is being
looked at they're paying attention to what

00:25:55.216 --> 00:25:56.647
you're saying-
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:25:56.647 --> 00:25:58.973
&gt;&gt; And interestingly the other people in

00:25:58.973 --> 00:26:01.451
the room don't feel neglected by that.

00:26:01.451 --> 00:26:04.654
As an audience member that's
a totally fine thing to watch.

00:26:04.654 --> 00:26:08.369
But as the person being looked at it,
it makes that very strong connection.

00:26:08.369 --> 00:26:12.751
&gt;&gt; And we're actually finding that
looking at the camera when doing virtual

00:26:12.751 --> 00:26:15.020
communication has a similar effect.

00:26:15.020 --> 00:26:18.250
So looking at the camera makes
the people on the other end feel like

00:26:18.250 --> 00:26:20.033
you're talking directly to them.

00:26:20.033 --> 00:26:21.368
&gt;&gt; Absolutely, and it's so

00:26:21.368 --> 00:26:24.828
hard to do because you're trained
to make eye contact with people,

00:26:24.828 --> 00:26:28.790
which virtually assures that you're
not looking directly into the camera.

00:26:28.790 --> 00:26:31.959
&gt;&gt; Right, so
let me ask you a question number two,

00:26:31.959 --> 00:26:35.059
who is a communicator that you admire and
why?

00:26:38.312 --> 00:26:42.576
Well, I don't normally think about
communicators that I admire or not,

00:26:42.576 --> 00:26:45.339
I guess I'm going to say I
give you a weird answer.

00:26:45.339 --> 00:26:47.346
I'm going to say Elizabeth Strout.

00:26:47.346 --> 00:26:50.582
&gt;&gt; Okay, I want more information.

00:26:50.582 --> 00:26:54.498
&gt;&gt; She's a novelist, we often don't
think of when we think of communicators,

00:26:54.498 --> 00:26:56.923
we often think of orators or
business leaders.

00:26:56.923 --> 00:27:01.745
I think novelists are clearly
communicators and what I like about her,

00:27:01.745 --> 00:27:06.483
she's a fabulous writer, but
good novelists revealed that they have

00:27:06.483 --> 00:27:09.640
very keen powers of empathy and
observation.

00:27:09.640 --> 00:27:14.807
So she writes about a person Olive
Kitteridge, who is a dislikable person,

00:27:14.807 --> 00:27:18.130
but she's the protagonist
of many of her books.

00:27:18.130 --> 00:27:21.084
And through placing you
in the mind of Olive,

00:27:21.084 --> 00:27:23.745
you gain this level of empathy with her.

00:27:23.745 --> 00:27:25.833
And so for
Elizabeth to be able to do that,

00:27:25.833 --> 00:27:28.938
she needs to both understand what
a person like that is like and

00:27:28.938 --> 00:27:32.482
then to be able to convey that to you
in a way that you empathize with it.

00:27:32.482 --> 00:27:35.853
And so I think to be a good communicator
you need to have keen powers of

00:27:35.853 --> 00:27:36.635
observation.

00:27:36.635 --> 00:27:39.425
You need to be able to read
the people in the audience and

00:27:39.425 --> 00:27:42.771
understand their reactions and
how they're responding to you.

00:27:42.771 --> 00:27:46.360
And you also need to have that empathy to
take their point of view to understand

00:27:46.360 --> 00:27:48.473
what they want to get
out of that interaction.

00:27:48.473 --> 00:27:52.309
And when novelists can do that, in a way,
particularly by depicting dislikeable

00:27:52.309 --> 00:27:56.218
people, as someone that you can still
identify with, I think is just incredible.

00:27:56.218 --> 00:28:00.296
&gt;&gt; I'm smiling because I'm thinking a good
novelist has to be able to read his or

00:28:00.296 --> 00:28:03.708
her audience so that the audience
in turn will read their stuff.

00:28:03.708 --> 00:28:06.544
So a lot of reading that
has to happen there.

00:28:06.544 --> 00:28:12.936
What are the first three ingredients that
go into a successful communication recipe?

00:28:12.936 --> 00:28:17.068
&gt;&gt; Okay, the first one I'll double up on
a bit, I guess I'll say attentiveness.

00:28:17.068 --> 00:28:19.693
You need to be attentive to the situation.

00:28:19.693 --> 00:28:22.174
Another one I would say
would be vulnerability.

00:28:22.174 --> 00:28:25.379
And the third one I would say is openness.

00:28:25.379 --> 00:28:31.058
I mean this in a way that's an orientation
toward the world of curiosity and

00:28:31.058 --> 00:28:32.511
lack of judgment.

00:28:32.511 --> 00:28:37.568
That if I'm really going to be engaging
in a successful interaction with you,

00:28:37.568 --> 00:28:42.702
I need to listen to the things that you're
saying with a sense of curiosity and

00:28:42.702 --> 00:28:44.045
a lack of judgment so

00:28:44.045 --> 00:28:48.775
that I can respond in a truly open way
&gt;&gt; Well Christian all of us have been

00:28:48.775 --> 00:28:53.814
listening to you with an intense
curiosity and we have learned so much.

00:28:53.814 --> 00:28:59.053
I thank you for sharing with us
your insights into communication.

00:28:59.053 --> 00:29:03.210
To my mind, what I heard you say as it,
it all boils down to approach,

00:29:03.210 --> 00:29:07.301
having an open approach,
seeking offers, being open to failure,

00:29:07.301 --> 00:29:10.531
really being attentive and
responsive to the needs of

00:29:10.531 --> 00:29:14.388
those you're communicating with
can make a huge difference.

00:29:14.388 --> 00:29:17.510
Thank you for your time and
thank you for your insights.

00:29:17.510 --> 00:29:18.504
&gt;&gt; Thanks, Pat.

